< Ad platforms and marketplaces show bidders a bid landscape, the estimated chance of winning at each candidate bid, and leave the choice to them or to their agents, which increasingly run on reasoning language models. We ask where the value of the reasoning tokens goes in that decision. Six small ($\le$14B) open models choose among 5--7 bids with empirical win curves from eBay prices, under forced budgets of up to 768 tokens, and are scored on held-out auctions against an exact calculator. They recover 42--85\% of the calculator's lead. Arithmetic slips are a large share of the remaining loss for one model only. In 56--92\% of it, the trace had already evaluated the best option and the answer chose another one, mostly the runner-up or the bid with the highest win probability, in a way that follows list position within the same scenario. A rule-based reader that does no arithmetic and takes the best of the expected profits the trace writes down recovers 72--97\% of the lead from the same tokens.
