< Much of the web's commerce is priced by automated auction, and platforms increasingly hand the bidder the forecast. Ad platforms and marketplaces expose a bid landscape or win-rate simulator, the estimated chance of winning at each candidate bid, and leave the choice of bid to the advertiser, seller or buyer, or to an agent acting for them. These choices are made per campaign, keyword or listing rather than per impression, so an agent can afford seconds of reasoning. LLM agents are being built to bid, price and negotiate in such markets \cite{chen2024aucarena,zhu2024synthetic,ahmed2026bazaar}, increasingly on reasoning models that spend tokens before acting, or with a tool for the arithmetic \cite{schick2023toolformer,gao2023pal,chen2023pot}. This Web Economics and Digital Society paper asks where the value of reasoning tokens is lost in the bid-landscape decision.
