< An option counts as \emph{evaluated} when the trace states a number within 2\% of its expected profit $(v-b)\hat p(b)$; a trace is \emph{complete} when all options are evaluated. Two LLM annotators, blind to this parser, annotated 150 random traces, and a third relabeled 60 and agreed on every option; against them the parser has 92\% precision and 97\% recall per option ($\kappa=0.70$). LLM labels are a check, not independent ground truth. The parser uses the true expected profits, so the \emph{oracle reader}, which takes the evaluated option with the highest expected profit, is a diagnostic upper bound, not a remedy. The \emph{text reader} does no arithmetic: it splits the trace at each option's mention, takes the last result number written in that segment, and picks the option with the largest; it acts once two options have values and otherwise keeps the model's answer. On the annotated traces it finds 96\% of the stated values and reads 95\% of them correctly.
---
> The \emph{text reader} does no arithmetic: it splits the trace at each option's mention and takes the last result number written in that option's segment, skipping the inputs. On 150 traces annotated by two LLM annotators who did not see our outputs (a third relabeled 60 and agreed on every option; LLM labels are a check, not ground truth), it finds 96\% of the stated values and reads 95\% correctly. A second parser marks an option \emph{evaluated} when the trace states its true expected profit within 2\% (92\% precision, 97\% recall per option against the annotators). An option's written value is its true value when the parser finds it and the reader's number otherwise, so a value counts as miscomputed only if the true one appears nowhere: 14\% of Granite-8B's written values and at most 3\% for the others, close to the annotators' 16\% and $\le$1\%.
