< Forcing 768 tokens raises $q$ by 0.11--0.18 (all intervals exclude zero after a Bonferroni correction), mostly between 512 and 768 tokens, and recovers 42--85\% of the calculator's lead (Table~\ref{tab:main}). The calculator stays ahead of every model in every bootstrap draw that also resamples the held-out auctions. The unforced run mostly measures more tokens, not natural stopping: 46--87\% of its traces reach the 1{,}024 cap for five models (Granite-8B always stops, by 669 tokens in 90\% of traces), and where a model stops before 768 tokens the forced trace scores within 0.02 of it. It recovers 72--96\% for five models but 49\% for Phi-mini. Dollar recovery is lower for five models because Cartier dominates the dollar gap (\$113--\$186 per decision at budget zero against \$2--\$15 elsewhere) and recovers least. The synthetic replication recovers more (47--98\%) although its traces finish less often; its menus spread expected profits more widely, and adding that spread to a pooled regression of the per-decision gain cuts the synthetic advantage from 0.088 ($p<0.001$) to 0.030 ($p=0.38$), about two thirds of it.
