< \paragraph{Most of the loss is at the answer, not the computation.}
< Table~\ref{tab:traces} splits the 768-token gap. Arithmetic slips that change which option the trace ranks first account for 26\% of DeepSeek-8B's gap and at most 11\% for the others; on the annotated traces they change the ranking in 4\% of traces. Running out of tokens before the best option is evaluated accounts for 6--39\%, most for Qwen3-8B and Granite-8B. In the rest, 56--92\% of the gap, the trace had already evaluated the best option and the answer took another: for the Qwen models mostly in traces cut off later (41--67\%), for Phi-mini and Granite-8B in complete traces (75\% and 52\%). A full comparison takes a median of 440--656 tokens with five options and 544--840 with seven (Figure~\ref{fig:main}b); completion by 768 tokens in the unforced trace matches the forced trace in 96--99.8\% of decisions. Completion does not just mark easy scenarios: within the same model and scenario, a complete trace scores 0.075 higher than an unfinished one, and the calculator scores 0.980 both on scenarios models usually finish and on those they do not.
---
> \paragraph{Most of the loss is after the computation.}
> Table~\ref{tab:traces} splits the 768-token gap using the trace's own written values. Arithmetic is 22\% of Granite-8B's gap and at most 5\% for the others. Options never reached account for 2--34\%, most for Qwen3-8B. In the remaining 62--93\% the trace had written the best option's value as the largest and the answer took another bid: in unfinished lists for the Qwen models (39--63\%), in finished ones for Phi-mini (82\%) and Granite-8B (61\%), and about equally for DeepSeek-8B. A full comparison takes a median of 440--656 tokens with five options and 544--840 with seven (Figure~\ref{fig:main}b), and completion by 768 tokens in the unforced trace matches the forced trace in 96--99.8\% of decisions. Within the same model and scenario, with budget fixed effects, a complete trace scores 0.075 higher than an unfinished one.
