< \paragraph{Answers miss the best option by list position.}
< Each scenario appears in three option orders, so the list position of the best option varies within a scenario. In complete traces, a conditional logit with scenario fixed effects shows that moving the best option from first to last raises the odds of passing over it for Phi-mini (12-fold [7, 21], 369 events) and the Qwen models (3.4 [1.3, 8.8], 35 [6, 197] and 14 [3, 54] for 4B, 8B and 14B; 52--85 events), lowers them for Granite-8B (0.06 [0.03, 0.13], 221 events), and has no clear effect for DeepSeek-8B (0.67 [0.28, 1.6]). The direction differs by model, as in multiple-choice benchmarks \cite{zheng2024mcq}. Passed-over answers are mostly the runner-up (29--68\%) or the highest-probability bid (19--56\%), and repeat the model's own budget-zero answer in 32--61\% of cases, against 17\% by chance and 21--33\% for correct answers. The free-text answer names the calculator's bid in at most 25\% of these traces, so this is not an artifact of the letter readout.
---
> \paragraph{Interruption and decision.}
> The \emph{incomplete} share is partly a cost of answering under a cap. When the best option is written and ranked first, the answer picks it in 45--89\% of complete traces but 14--62\% of unfinished ones, and in unfinished traces this rate does not rise with the tokens written since the best option's value (logit $p=0.06$, slope negative). The models rarely stop early on their own, and when they stop before the cap they are right in at least 97.6\% of cases, so the unforced run cannot separate the two for them. Granite-8B stops on its own in every trace and shows the same pattern without forcing: of its unforced gap, 55\% is passed over, 27\% arithmetic and 14\% unreached.
136,137c134,135
< \paragraph{A text reader recovers most of the rest.}
< The text reader recovers 72--97\% of the gap at 768 tokens from the same tokens, against 42--85\% for the model's answer, and 66--88\% at 512 tokens, where the answer recovers 17--56\% (Table~\ref{tab:main}, Figure~\ref{fig:main}c). It never lowers $q$ by more than 0.025 at any budget, and the stricter rule that waits for $K-1$ values never by more than 0.028. It gains most for Phi-mini (42\% to 91\%), whose traces finish but whose answers do not follow them, and least for Granite-8B (65\% to 72\%), where 38\% of the gap is options never reached. The oracle reader bounds what reading can add (81--100\%). Reading the trace's stated conclusion with our patterns does worse than the model's answer for five models. Sampling is no substitute for length: majority votes (ties broken by summed option probability) of two 384-token or five 128-token samples score 0.05--0.19 below one 768-token sample.
---
> \paragraph{Passing over follows list position, not only near-ties.}
> Each scenario appears in three option orders, so the best option's list position varies within a scenario while its difficulty does not. In complete traces, a conditional logit with scenario fixed effects shows that moving the best option from first to last raises the odds of passing over it for Phi-mini (12-fold [7, 21], 369 events) and the Qwen models (3.4 [1.3, 8.8], 35 [6, 197] and 14 [3, 54]; 52--85 events), lowers them for Granite-8B (0.06 [0.03, 0.13], 221 events), and has no clear effect for DeepSeek-8B (0.67 [0.28, 1.6]). On the synthetic auctions the two models with the largest passed shares repeat the pattern (Phi-mini 17-fold [6, 48], Granite-8B 0.09 [0.02, 0.39]); the others have too few events. For DeepSeek-8B and the Qwen models, 54--63\% of passed-over cases are near-ties (best and runner-up within 2\%, against 22--27\% of cases overall), so many of these cost little; for Phi-mini and Granite-8B the near-tie share is close to the base rate (26\% vs 24\%, 37\% vs 30\%). Passed-over answers repeat the model's budget-zero answer in 32--61\% of cases, against about 20\% by chance and 21--33\% for correct answers. The free-text answer names the calculator's bid in at most 25\% of these traces, so this is not an artifact of the letter readout.
