< \paragraph{Spending tokens selectively.}
< A gradient-boosted router, trained with nested cross-validation grouped by value family, beats a random mix of 0 and 768 tokens by 0.012--0.031 in $q$ at equal spend within known products; with three product categories, we cannot show that it transfers to a new one (areas 0.31--0.58, random 0.50).
---
> \paragraph{Wrong answers overbid.}
> At 768 tokens, 81--91\% of the wrong answers of DeepSeek-8B and the Qwen models bid above the calculator, by 12--20\% of the private value on average; for Phi-mini and Granite-8B the figure is 60\% and 51\%. Averaged over all decisions, expected payment to the seller is \$18--\$83 above the calculator's choice while the win probability differs by at most 4 points, so most of the lost profit is paid to sellers rather than buying a better chance of winning. Lost expected profit is 2.0--5.2\% of the private value for Cartier, 0.3--3.8\% for Xbox and at most 0.8\% for Palm.
142,143c140,144
< \paragraph{Costs.}
< A calculator call costs nothing beyond the request. Reasoning tokens are cheap too: 768 tokens pay over budget zero whenever a decision's best expected profit exceeds a few cents (Table~\ref{tab:main}), and below \$0.35 even at \$50 per million tokens, the top of current API list prices \cite{openai2026pricing,anthropic2026pricing,google2026pricing,deepseek2026pricing}. The cost that counts is the value left behind: the models still lose \$15--\$36 of expected profit per decision against the calculator.
---
> \paragraph{Reading the trace.}
> Taking the option with the largest value the trace wrote, once two values exist, recovers 73--96\% of the gap at 768 tokens and 65--88\% at 512, where the answer recovers 17--56\%; it never lowers $q$ by more than 0.026 at any budget, and changes by at most 4 points for segment windows of 300--1{,}000 characters (Table~\ref{tab:main}, Figure~\ref{fig:main}c). Waiting for $K-1$ values gives 73--92\%. The oracle reader, which uses true values and acts on any evaluated option, falls below the answer at 256 tokens and bounds the reader only once most options are evaluated. On the synthetic traces, which we did not use to design the reader, it helps Phi-mini and Granite-8B (47\% to 86\%, 65\% to 73\%) but lowers recovery by 3--14 points for the other four, whose answers already track their traces. The reader is therefore better as an audit than as a replacement: it disagrees with the answer in 18--57\% of 768-token decisions, and the answer is wrong in 58--89\% of those. Where the agent can call a calculator, the calculator is better still; the audit applies where a platform or user can read an agent's trace but not change its tools.
> 
> \paragraph{Routing and costs.}
> A gradient-boosted router, trained with nested cross-validation grouped by value family, beats a random mix of 0 and 768 tokens by 0.012--0.031 in $q$ at equal spend within known products, but we cannot show that it transfers to a new product (areas 0.31--0.58, random 0.50). Tokens are cheap: 768 tokens pay over budget zero once a decision's best expected profit exceeds a few cents (Table~\ref{tab:main}), and below \$0.35 even at \$50 per million tokens, the top of current API list prices \cite{openai2026pricing,anthropic2026pricing,google2026pricing,deepseek2026pricing}. The cost that counts is the value left behind, \$15--\$36 of expected profit per decision, mostly on Cartier.
